WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.227
First 90 Days at a New Job.

2
00:00:02.507 --> 00:00:06.539
Day 85 of the Soft Skill Playbook course, in the Career phase.

3
00:00:08.289 --> 00:00:09.313
The key idea.

4
00:00:09.593 --> 00:00:12.729
First impressions are formed in weeks and last for years.

5
00:00:13.009 --> 00:00:18.812
Spend the first 90 days listening, building relationships, and delivering one visible early win.

6
00:00:20.162 --> 00:00:21.015
Part 1.

7
00:00:21.295 --> 00:00:23.322
The 30 to 60 to 90 plan.

8
00:00:24.572 --> 00:00:25.831
Days 1 to 30:

9
00:00:26.111 --> 00:00:29.524
Learn: people, product, codebase, how decisions get made.

10
00:00:29.804 --> 00:00:31.447
Days 31 to 60:

11
00:00:31.727 --> 00:00:35.695
Contribute: ship real work, join discussions, own something small.

12
00:00:35.975 --> 00:00:37.617
Days 61 to 90:

13
00:00:37.897 --> 00:00:41.545
Lead: propose improvements, own a feature, mentor where you can.

14
00:00:42.895 --> 00:00:43.770
Part 2.

15
00:00:44.050 --> 00:00:45.351
Introducing yourself.

16
00:00:46.601 --> 00:00:48.884
To the team (stand-up or first meeting).

17
00:00:49.164 --> 00:00:50.103
You could say:

18
00:00:50.383 --> 00:00:52.004
Hi everyone, I'm Ashish.

19
00:00:52.284 --> 00:00:54.737
I've joined as a senior backend engineer.

20
00:00:55.017 --> 00:00:59.177
Before this, I spent 6 years building payment systems at XYZ.

21
00:00:59.457 --> 00:01:01.868
Outside work, I love cricket and cooking.

22
00:01:02.148 --> 00:01:06.756
I'm looking forward to learning how things work here, so please bear with my questions!

23
00:01:08.106 --> 00:01:09.557
Slack or Teams intro.

24
00:01:09.837 --> 00:01:11.714
Here is a template you can copy.

25
00:01:11.994 --> 00:01:12.890
Hi all!

26
00:01:13.170 --> 00:01:17.095
I'm Ashish, joining the Checkout team as a Senior Backend Engineer.

27
00:01:17.375 --> 00:01:18.314
Previously:

28
00:01:18.594 --> 00:01:22.434
6 yrs on payments at XYZ (Java, Kafka, Postgres).

29
00:01:22.714 --> 00:01:23.653
Fun fact:

30
00:01:23.933 --> 00:01:26.343
I've visited 14 states on train journeys.

31
00:01:26.623 --> 00:01:29.205
Would love a 15-min coffee chat with anyone.

32
00:01:29.485 --> 00:01:31.298
Happy to learn how your team works!

33
00:01:34.148 --> 00:01:36.324
To your new manager (first one-on-one).

34
00:01:36.604 --> 00:01:40.209
What does success look like for me at 30, 60 and 90 days?

35
00:01:40.489 --> 00:01:42.196
How do you prefer to communicate:

36
00:01:42.476 --> 00:01:44.097
Slack, email, one-on-ones?

37
00:01:44.377 --> 00:01:46.063
How often do you want updates?

38
00:01:46.343 --> 00:01:49.201
Who are the key people I should meet in my first month?

39
00:01:49.481 --> 00:01:52.127
What's the biggest challenge the team faces right now?

40
00:01:52.407 --> 00:01:55.969
Is there a small, valuable task I could pick up as a first win?

41
00:01:57.319 --> 00:01:58.215
Part 3.

42
00:01:58.495 --> 00:02:00.394
Questions to ask in your first month.

43
00:02:01.644 --> 00:02:02.988
Here is a quick table:

44
00:02:03.268 --> 00:02:04.591
Area, and Questions.

45
00:02:04.871 --> 00:02:05.745
Product:

46
00:02:06.025 --> 00:02:07.220
Who are our users?

47
00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:09.185
What do they complain about most?

48
00:02:09.465 --> 00:02:10.383
Codebase:

49
00:02:10.663 --> 00:02:12.028
Which parts are fragile?

50
00:02:12.308 --> 00:02:13.865
Where is the documentation?

51
00:02:14.145 --> 00:02:15.105
Process:

52
00:02:15.385 --> 00:02:17.753
How does code go from PR to production?

53
00:02:18.033 --> 00:02:19.441
What's the on-call setup?

54
00:02:19.721 --> 00:02:20.511
People:

55
00:02:20.791 --> 00:02:23.969
Who's the go-to person for payments or infra or data?

56
00:02:24.249 --> 00:02:25.060
Culture:

57
00:02:25.340 --> 00:02:26.684
How are decisions made?

58
00:02:26.964 --> 00:02:28.607
How do people disagree here?

59
00:02:28.887 --> 00:02:29.719
History:

60
00:02:29.999 --> 00:02:31.556
Why was it built this way?

61
00:02:31.836 --> 00:02:34.119
(ask out of curiosity, not to judge).

62
00:02:35.469 --> 00:02:36.386
Part 4.

63
00:02:36.666 --> 00:02:39.162
Senior hires: avoid the classic trap.

64
00:02:40.412 --> 00:02:41.159
Warning:

65
00:02:41.439 --> 00:02:45.599
Don't start with \At my last company we did it better...\.

66
00:02:45.879 --> 00:02:48.439
Even when you're right, it sounds like criticism.

67
00:02:48.719 --> 00:02:49.465
Try:

68
00:02:49.745 --> 00:02:52.369
Help me understand why we chose this approach.

69
00:02:52.649 --> 00:02:55.209
I've seen a pattern that worked well for this problem.

70
00:02:55.489 --> 00:02:57.260
Would it be useful if I wrote it up?

71
00:02:57.540 --> 00:03:02.063
Earn trust with small wins first; propose big changes after 60 days.

72
00:03:03.413 --> 00:03:04.373
Part 5.

73
00:03:04.653 --> 00:03:06.509
Habits for the first 90 days.

74
00:03:07.759 --> 00:03:11.961
Take notes in every meeting; start a personal how things work here doc.

75
00:03:12.241 --> 00:03:13.265
Coffee chats:

76
00:03:13.545 --> 00:03:15.636
2 per week with people across teams.

77
00:03:15.916 --> 00:03:20.161
Fix the onboarding docs wherever you hit a gap (an easy first PR).

78
00:03:20.441 --> 00:03:23.556
Ship your first PR in week 1, even if it's tiny.

79
00:03:23.836 --> 00:03:27.079
Send a weekly update to your manager for the first 2 months.

80
00:03:27.359 --> 00:03:29.065
Ask for feedback at day 30:

81
00:03:29.345 --> 00:03:30.476
How am I doing?

82
00:03:30.756 --> 00:03:32.164
Anything I should adjust?

83
00:03:32.444 --> 00:03:36.476
Remember names: say them back when introduced, and write them down after.

84
00:03:37.826 --> 00:03:38.765
Part 6.

85
00:03:39.045 --> 00:03:41.562
Day-90 conversation with your manager.

86
00:03:42.812 --> 00:03:43.751
You could say:

87
00:03:44.031 --> 00:03:46.953
It's been 90 days, and I'd like to reflect together.

88
00:03:47.233 --> 00:03:51.201
Here's what I've delivered: the coupon validator and the on-call runbook.

89
00:03:51.481 --> 00:03:53.167
Here's what I've learned about the team.

90
00:03:53.447 --> 00:03:57.415
What's your feedback, and what should my priorities be for the next 6 months?

91
00:03:58.765 --> 00:03:59.703
Part 7.

92
00:03:59.983 --> 00:04:01.370
Practice conversation:

93
00:04:01.650 --> 00:04:03.869
First one-on-one with your new manager.

94
00:04:05.119 --> 00:04:07.145
Here is how the conversation goes.

95
00:04:07.425 --> 00:04:08.513
Welcome!

96
00:04:08.793 --> 00:04:10.457
How's the first week going?

97
00:04:10.737 --> 00:04:12.103
Really well, thanks.

98
00:04:12.383 --> 00:04:14.345
Everyone's been very helpful.

99
00:04:14.625 --> 00:04:15.884
Glad to hear it.

100
00:04:16.164 --> 00:04:17.743
What questions do you have?

101
00:04:18.023 --> 00:04:22.396
What would success look like for me after 30, 60 and 90 days?

102
00:04:22.676 --> 00:04:25.684
In 30 days, learn the codebase and ship a small fix.

103
00:04:25.964 --> 00:04:28.268
By 90 days, own the coupon service.

104
00:04:29.618 --> 00:04:31.218
The conversation continues.

105
00:04:31.498 --> 00:04:32.522
That's clear.

106
00:04:32.802 --> 00:04:34.871
How do you prefer to get updates:

107
00:04:35.151 --> 00:04:37.157
Slack, email or one-on-ones?

108
00:04:37.437 --> 00:04:40.786
Slack for quick things, and a short written update every Friday.

109
00:04:41.066 --> 00:04:41.962
Perfect.

110
00:04:42.242 --> 00:04:44.994
Who are the key people I should meet this month?

111
00:04:45.274 --> 00:04:47.791
Meena in payments and Arun in DevOps.

112
00:04:48.071 --> 00:04:49.415
I'll introduce you.

113
00:04:50.765 --> 00:04:52.301
That's the end of this lesson.

114
00:04:52.581 --> 00:04:57.232
Pick one script from it, say it out loud three times, and use it for real today.

115
00:04:57.512 --> 00:05:03.635
For extra practice, paste the Master Prompt into an AI assistant and role-play the conversation.

